@AlternativPerspectiv the reason the EU is not happy about chinese subsidies to EVs is that they’re direct per unit produced just to make them compete even better against foreign brands. All while forcing foreign brands to partner up with local manufacturers to produce and sell in the country (with the risk to IP protection that it entails). 

China has been applying such rules for about 20 years, and here you are complaining about Europe reacting against an invasion of cheap cars, the result of very likely shady IP inspiration and direct government subsidies on production. All for a country under a dictatorship, whose workers are paid low wages and work under conditions unacceptable in Europe.

So yeah, you get a more expensive car, deal with it or buy a bicycle

